{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "process-airtoxics-runspec",
    "description": "PLAN.md §7.5 step 7d: the `process-airtoxics` RunSpec -- THC running exhaust, the two hydrocarbon species MOVES speciates it into on the way, and the three gaseous air toxics it chains off VOC -- computed from the DEFAULT DATABASE. It is the thirteenth RunSpec this chain reproduces and the first that needs a table the default database ships EMPTY and MOVES writes at run time from two different generators at once.\n\nWHAT IT DISCRIMINATES (§23).\n\n* `ATRatio` IS EMPTY IN THE DEFAULT DATABASE, AND ITS TWO HALVES ARE TWO DIFFERENT COMPUTATIONS. `FuelEffectsGenerator` fills it, and the snapshot path this repository used until now read the filled table. For model years 2001 and later the rows are `generalFuelRatioExpression` evaluated on the county's supplied formulations -- three algebraic forms docs/fuel-effects-generator.md 2.1 counted and recorded as NOT ported, because every snapshot that carries them hands them off before capture. For model years 2000 and earlier they are a quotient of two COMPLEX MODEL ratios against the `airtoxicsA` base fuel, weighted by what that base fuel emits. The same split `criteriaRatio` has, at a fourth calculation engine.\n\n* A CONSTANT THAT TURNS OUT TO BE A KEY. The complex model read `meanFuelParameters` and `baseFuel` at the literal model-year group 19502000, which is right for all three criteria configurations and wrong for all four air-toxics ones -- they sit at group 0 and their base fuel is formulation 96 rather than 99. §47's width-one trap, found by the second engine and not by the first three, and it is invisible in the criteria answer because it moves nothing there.\n\n* CENTRING ONLY SHOWS UP IN A NONLINEAR TERM. `meanFuelParameters` centres every complex-model property, and a centre CANCELS in `exp(target) - exp(base)` whenever the term is linear in it. Benzene, 1,3-butadiene and formaldehyde use linear terms only and reproduce to 3e-08 with the centring silently missing; VOC, their shared denominator, has an E200 squared, an E300 squared and an E300-times-aromatics term, and without the centring every one of the three toxic ratios is 7.4 percent high. A bug that three of four configurations cannot see is the argument for checking an INTERMEDIATE and not only an answer.\n\n* TWO RATIO TABLES ARE APPENDED, NOT CHOSEN BETWEEN. `atratio` keys on the fuel FORMULATION and `atrationongas` on the fuel SUBTYPE and the SOURCE TYPE, and `airtoxics.rs` runs both paths and appends both scaled emissions. They are disjoint on every cohort this run emits -- gasoline and E85 from 2001 take the first, diesel and E85 before 2001 the second -- so the sum has one live term, which is a measurement and not a licence to write the narrower form.\n\n* A ZERO RATIO IS A ROW. 1,3-butadiene's model-year-2001 expression ends in `*(0.0)` and diesel's benzene and butadiene ratios are 0 from 2010. 60 of this run's 644 cells are exactly zero and every one of them is compared: an absent ratio drops a row, a zero ratio emits one, and §30.1's difference between the two is what the count checks.\n\n644 rows, worst cell 7.564e-06. Reproduced in pandas from the DEFAULT database before the port -- including both halves of the generated `ATRatio` -- at NMHC 7.892e-06, VOC 7.422e-06, benzene 5.295e-06, 1,3-butadiene 5.083e-06 and formaldehyde 7.325e-06.",
    "authors": [
      "EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"
    ],
    "tags": [
      "runspec",
      "onroad",
      "default-database",
      "chain-tog-speciation",
      "hc-speciation",
      "chained",
      "ragged",
      "fixed-point"
    ]
  },
